    {
      "page_title": "Hash Slots: The 16384 Buckets",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "array",
          "spec": {
            "cells": [
              "0",
              "1",
              "…",
              "5460",
              "5461",
              "…",
              "10922",
              "16383"
            ],
            "caption": "slot space 0..16383"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Ownership Rule"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every master owns a set of slots — contiguous or not",
            "Union of all owned slots must be 16384",
            "One slot uncovered and the cluster is down"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Master",
            "Slots",
            "Count"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "A",
              "0-5460",
              "5461"
            ],
            [
              "B",
              "5461-10922",
              "5462"
            ],
            [
              "C",
              "10923-16383",
              "5461"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why slots instead of a consistent hashing ring?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Slots are a small fixed integer space",
            "Ownership fits in a 2 KB bitmap",
            "That bitmap is gossiped in every heartbeat"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Why 16384, Not 65536",
          "text": "The slot bitmap rides in every gossip packet. 16384 bits = 2 KB; 65536 bits = 8 KB. Redis targets ~1000 nodes max, so the bigger space buys nothing."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Migration Unit"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Slots are the unit of migration, not keys. You reshard by moving slot ownership, then the keys that live inside it."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Debug Toolkit"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "CLUSTER KEYSLOT mykey      # 14687",
            "CLUSTER COUNTKEYSINSLOT 14687",
            "CLUSTER GETKEYSINSLOT 14687 10",
            "CLUSTER SHARDS             # 7.0+ map"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`CLUSTER SLOTS` is the legacy client-facing map",
            "`CLUSTER SHARDS` (7.0+) also reports replica health",
            "Each node keeps its own bitmap plus its view of everyone else's, in `clusterState`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Eventually Consistent Map",
          "items": [
            "Slot-to-node view propagates by gossip",
            "A stale view is what produces MOVED",
            "Empty masters (zero slots) are legal",
            "Add a node empty, then assign slots"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Gotcha",
          "text": "Even slot distribution does not mean even key distribution. One hot slot can hold a 10 GB list while its 5460 neighbours hold nothing."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Definition",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "If a key contains `{...}` with a non-empty substring between the first `{` and the first `}` after it, only that substring is hashed."
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "user:{1000}:profile",
              "extract `1000`",
              "CRC16",
              "mod 16384",
              "slot 1649"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Hash Tags: Forcing Keys Together",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`user:{1000}:profile` and `user:{1000}:sessions` co-locate",
            "Both hash on `1000`, so both land in one slot",
            "Same slot means MULTI, Lua and MGET work on them"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Parsing Edge Cases"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Key",
            "Hashed part",
            "Why"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`{a}{b}`",
              "`a`",
              "first pair wins"
            ],
            [
              "`foo{}bar`",
              "`foo{}bar`",
              "empty tag ignored"
            ],
            [
              "`foo{bar`",
              "`foo{bar`",
              "unclosed brace"
            ],
            [
              "`{user1}x`",
              "`user1`",
              "normal case"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Silent Bug",
          "text": "`{` immediately followed by `}` falls back to full-key hashing. Your templating produced an empty id and co-location quietly vanished."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Design Pattern"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Tag by tenant, user, or entity id",
            "Pick your domain's natural transaction boundary",
            "Tags are the ONLY supported co-location guarantee",
            "Deliberate skew is fine for a small tenant"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Anti-Pattern"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "SET {prod}:orders:1 ...   # cardinality 1",
            "SET {prod}:orders:2 ...   # same slot",
            "# every key on ONE node forever"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Hot Tag, No Escape",
          "items": [
            "One overloaded tag = one hot slot",
            "Slots are indivisible — resharding cannot split it",
            "Rebalance moves the problem, never fixes it",
            "Real fix: schema change + data migration"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Check Before Shipping"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "CLUSTER KEYSLOT 'user:{1000}:profile'",
            "CLUSTER KEYSLOT '{1000}'",
            "# must print the same number"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Lua Depends On This",
          "text": "Scripts must declare every touched key in `KEYS[]`, and all of them must share a slot. Hash tags are how you satisfy that rule."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Any Node, One Owner",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Every node accepts every command, but serves only keys in slots it owns. Otherwise it answers with a redirect error."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Error",
            "Meaning",
            "Client action"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "MOVED",
              "permanent",
              "update map, retry"
            ],
            [
              "ASK",
              "one key, once",
              "ASKING, do NOT cache"
            ],
            [
              "TRYAGAIN",
              "keys split",
              "backoff, retry"
            ],
            [
              "CLUSTERDOWN",
              "slot uncovered",
              "fail the request"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "-MOVED 3999 127.0.0.1:6381",
            "-ASK   3999 127.0.0.1:6381"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Client Redirection: MOVED, ASK, Smart Clients",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The ASK Handshake"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "GET k",
              "-ASK 3999",
              "ASKING",
              "GET k on target",
              "value"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Two commands, same connection, to the target node",
            "`ASKING` sets a one-shot flag on that node",
            "The flag lets it serve a slot it is only IMPORTING",
            "Never update your slot map from an ASK"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Dumb client",
                "lines": [
                  "connects to one node",
                  "follows every redirect",
                  "extra round trip most requests"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Smart client",
                "lines": [
                  "reads CLUSTER SHARDS at startup",
                  "keeps a slot to node table",
                  "right node on the first try"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When To Refresh"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "On a MOVED reply",
            "On a connection error to a known node",
            "On a periodic timer, low frequency"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Redirect Storm",
          "text": "A client that refetches the whole map on every single MOVED will hammer the cluster through a large reshard. Debounce it."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reading From Replicas"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "READONLY    # serve reads here",
            "GET user:{1000}:profile",
            "READWRITE   # back to redirects"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`READONLY` stops a replica redirecting reads to its master",
            "Replica data is asynchronous and may be stale",
            "You trade consistency for read throughput, explicitly"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Check Your Library",
          "text": "Client maturity matters more than server config. Verify yours implements ASK plus ASKING, not only MOVED — half-support breaks mid-reshard."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The One Rule",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Every key in one command must live in the same hash slot. Otherwise: `CROSSSLOT Keys in request don't hash to the same slot`."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "MGET a b            # CROSSSLOT error",
            "MGET {t}:a {t}:b    # fine, one slot"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Commands That Trip It",
          "items": [
            "`MSET`, `MGET`, multi-arg `DEL`",
            "`SUNION`, `SINTER`, `ZUNIONSTORE`",
            "`RENAME`, `SMOVE`, `BITOP`, `PFMERGE`"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Same Slot, Not Same Node",
          "text": "Two slots that happen to sit on one master are still rejected. The check is on the slot number, never on the address."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Multi-Key Limits: What Cluster Takes Away",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Scripting And Transactions"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`MULTI`/`EXEC` works only if all keys share a slot",
            "`EVAL`: every `KEYS[i]` in one slot",
            "Touching an undeclared key is undefined, breaks on migration",
            "Redis 7 Functions (`FCALL`) follow the same rule"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The MGET Workaround"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "key list",
              "group by slot",
              "pipeline per node",
              "merge in app"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Most smart clients do this fan-out for you. Check whether yours groups keys or simply throws CROSSSLOT at you."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Per-Node Commands"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Command",
            "Scope",
            "Consequence"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`SCAN`",
              "one node",
              "cursors not portable"
            ],
            [
              "`KEYS`",
              "one node",
              "one shard only"
            ],
            [
              "`DBSIZE`",
              "one node",
              "wrong total"
            ],
            [
              "`FLUSHALL`",
              "one node",
              "partial wipe"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Iterate every master to see the whole keyspace",
            "A monitor hitting one node reports one shard",
            "No `SELECT`: cluster mode has database 0 only",
            "Multi-tenant DB-index tricks become key prefixes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pub/Sub And Blocking"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "SSUBSCRIBE room:{42}   # 7.0 sharded",
            "SPUBLISH  room:{42} hi # stays on shard"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pre-7.0 Pub/Sub broadcasts cluster-wide, costing every node bandwidth",
            "Sharded Pub/Sub routes by the channel name's slot",
            "`BLPOP` works, but only on keys in one slot",
            "Cross-shard work queues need per-shard workers"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Design Consequence",
          "text": "Model your atomic groups behind a shared hash tag BEFORE you migrate to Cluster. Retrofitting tags means rewriting keys under load."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Resharding: Moving Slots Without Downtime",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Resharding Is",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Transferring ownership of slots, and the keys inside them, from a source master to a target master while both serve live traffic."
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "IMPORTING",
              "MIGRATING",
              "GETKEYSINSLOT",
              "MIGRATE",
              "SETSLOT NODE"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Four Steps"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "# target:",
            "CLUSTER SETSLOT 99 IMPORTING <src-id>",
            "# source:",
            "CLUSTER SETSLOT 99 MIGRATING <dst-id>"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "During migration the source serves keys it still holds",
            "For keys already moved it answers `-ASK`",
            "Clients keep working the whole time"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "MIGRATE Is Atomic Per Batch",
          "text": "DUMP-serialize, send, RESTORE on target, DELETE on source. The key exists on exactly one node at every instant — never both, never neither."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "But It Blocks",
          "text": "MIGRATE stalls both nodes for the batch duration. One 2 GB list freezes the server. Keep batch sizes small."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Driving It With redis-cli"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "redis-cli --cluster reshard h:6379",
            "redis-cli --cluster rebalance h:6379 \\",
            "  --cluster-use-empty-masters"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Task",
            "Order"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "add node",
              "`add-node`, then reshard onto it"
            ],
            [
              "remove node",
              "reshard away, then `del-node`"
            ],
            [
              "repair",
              "`check`, then `fix`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A new node starts with zero slots and serves nothing",
            "Deleting a node that still owns slots breaks the cluster",
            "`--cluster-use-empty-masters` includes fresh nodes in a rebalance"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Stuck Slot",
          "items": [
            "Symptom: slot left MIGRATING/IMPORTING after a crash",
            "`--cluster check` detects the open slot",
            "`--cluster fix` clears the flag or completes the move"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "What does a reshard actually cost?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "O(keys in slot) network transfer",
            "O(1) ownership metadata update",
            "Throughput-limited by MIGRATE round trips"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Resharding: Moving Slots Without Downtime (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Rule",
          "text": "Never reshard during a failover or with any node in a fail state. Finish one topology change before you begin the next."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Cluster Bus",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Nodes gossip on client port + 10000",
            "Binary protocol, separate from the client port",
            "Firewall both ports or the cluster never forms"
          ]
